/konstructio/konstruct-ui/commits/a43c03d53b442bb9d256478dcad7217c28dd0d02"}],"sha":"0cd4df8f34f5cafc87e50d9994b0926a97de046c","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/konstructio/konstruct-ui/commits/0cd4df8f34f5cafc87e50d9994b0926a97de046c"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3932407?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jairoFernandez/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jairoFernandez/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jairoFernandez/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jairoFernandez/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/jairoFernandez","id":3932407,"login":"jairoFernandez","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM5MzI0MDc=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jairoFernandez/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jairoFernandez/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jairoFernandez/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jairoFernandez/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jairoFernandez/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/jairoFernandez","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/konstructio/konstruct-ui/commits/a43c03d53b442bb9d256478dcad7217c28dd0d02/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-03T15:06:21Z","email":"jairofg12@gmail.com","name":"Jairo Fernández"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-03T15:06:21Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(DateRangePicker): consumer-supplied presets, opt-in apply-on-select and progressive disclosure (#710)\n\n* ✨ feat: let DateRangePicker consumers supply their own presets\n\nThe preset panel offered five hardcoded options, and the range each one stood\nfor lived in a separate switch in `calculatePresetRange`. A consumer could\nrename them through `presetLabels` but not change what they meant, so a design\nasking for rolling windows — \"Last 24 hours\", \"the past 7×24 hours\" — could not\nbe expressed at all: `current-month` and `today` are calendar buckets, and\n`last-7-days` is seven whole days ending at midnight.\n\nAn option now owns both halves. `presets` takes a list of\n`{ value, label, resolve(now) }`, the built-ins carry their own resolvers, and\nthe switch is gone — the panel and the resolution can no longer drift apart.\nPassing `now` in is what makes a rolling window expressible.\n\n`custom` stays the reserved id the calendar switches back to when a day is\npicked; an option whose `resolve` returns an empty range is treated as that\nmanual-selection entry, so a consumer''s list keeps working without knowing the\ninternals. Array order is render order, which also lets \"Custom range\" sit last.\n\n`DateRangePreset` widens to accept any id while keeping autocompletion for the\nfive built-ins, so no existing consumer breaks. `presetLabels` still works and\nis documented as superseded by `presets`.\n\nAlso forwards `presets` through `VirtualizedTable`''s `dateRange` filter config\nand `DateRangeFilterDropdown`. Without that the table — the main way this picker\nis consumed — would still be stuck with the built-in five.\n\nTests cover the new prop (supplied options replace the built-ins, render order,\nresolution through the option''s own `resolve`, `now` being passed, the\nempty-range option behaving as manual selection, `defaultPreset` resolving\nagainst a supplied list) plus a guard that the built-in windows are unchanged\nafter moving them out of the switch. A RollingPresets story is added to both\nthemes.\n\n* ✨ feat: opt-in apply-on-preset and labels for the date range filter\n\nFollow-up to the presets prop, from reviewing a product''s local reimplementation\nof this filter against the design it was built to. Three of its five departures\nfrom the library were left alone on purpose: closing on apply already exists as\n`closeOnApply`, an inverted range is a deliberate error message here\n(`errorStartAfterEnd`) rather than a silent swap, and progressively revealing the\ncalendar would restructure this component''s layout. Only the additive parts are\ntaken.\n\n- `onPresetChange(preset, range)` on DateRangePicker: fires whenever a preset is\n  chosen, including a manual-selection entry resolving to nothing. `onRangeChange`\n  cannot distinguish \"chose a preset\" from \"clicked a day\", and a consumer needs\n  that distinction to react to one and not the other. Purely additive.\n\n- `applyOnPresetSelect` on the filter dropdown (default false, so nothing\n  changes): applies and closes as soon as a preset is picked, while the calendar\n  still waits for the button. A design that treats presets as one-click shortcuts\n  can opt in.\n\n- The apply button is disabled while nothing is selected. Applying an empty range\n  filtered nothing and reported a range that was never chosen; clearing is what\n  the reset button is for.\n\n- `labelApply` / `labelReset` on the dropdown and the table''s filter config. Both\n  strings were hardcoded English in a library shipped to several products, so\n  this is as much an i18n gap as a wording one.\n\nAll four are forwarded through VirtualizedTable''s `dateRange` filter config.\n\n* ✨ feat: opt-in progressive disclosure for the date range calendar\n\nThird and last idea taken from a product''s local reimplementation of this\nfilter: presets as one-click shortcuts, with the calendar revealed only when the\nuser asks for a custom range. Reviewing that side by side, its dropdown reads\nbetter than always showing two months for a choice most users make with a\npreset — so the behaviour is offered rather than imposed. Default is unchanged.\n\n- `revealCalendarOnCustom` on DateRangePicker (default false): the date inputs\n  and calendar render only while the manual-selection preset is active — the\n  entry whose `resolve` returns an empty range, `custom` by default. Keyed on\n  the resolved range rather than the literal id, so a consumer''s own preset list\n  works without knowing the internals.\n\n  Pair it with a `defaultPreset` that resolves to a window: the library defaults\n  to `custom`, which would otherwise open expanded. Documented on the prop.\n\n- A `RightPanel` subcomponent now wraps the inputs + calendar. Whether the\n  active preset resolves to a window is only knowable from context, and the\n  provider is created by DateRangePicker itself, so the decision has to live in\n  a child.\n\n- Forwarded through the filter dropdown and VirtualizedTable''s `dateRange`\n  config, plus `labelTimePeriod`, which the dropdown accepted nowhere before —\n  the panel title was stuck on \"Time period\" with no way to say \"Created\".\n\nTests cover both states and the transitions in each direction, plus that the\npresets stay visible while the calendar is hidden and that the default keeps the\ncalendar on screen. A RevealOnCustom story is added to both themes, and the\nfilter dropdown gets its first story (CreatedDateRange) so the assembled control\ncan be reviewed rather than only its parts.\n\n* 💄 refactor: align the date range preset panel with the design system\n\nThe preset column was a translucent card floating inside the picker: it\ncarried its own fill and rounded corners, and sat inset from the edge by the\ncontainer''s padding. The design has one continuous surface split by hairlines,\nso the padding moves from the container onto each column and the gap between\nthem becomes a border. The container already declared a border colour it never\ndrew; that is now the token both rules use.\n\nThe options themselves read as a list rather than a set of radios. The control\nis hidden instead of removed, so the rows keep their radio semantics and\nkeyboard handling, and selection shows as a trailing check. A manual-selection\noption that closes the list is marked with a chevron and ruled off from the\nrolling presets above it — the rule is a pseudo-element, since it runs\nfull-bleed while the row''s hover fill stays inset.\n\nSizes come from the Figma node: a fixed 206px column so the calendar beside it\ndoes not shift with the labels, 24px to the text, a 36px row on a 40px pitch,\nand 20px icons.\n\n* ✨ feat: From/To labels and required marks for the date range filter\n\nThe filter dropdown never forwarded the picker''s date labels, so a consumer\nhad no way to reach the From/To wording the design asks for inside a filter.\nIt forwards them now and defaults to that wording, and the reset action is\ncalled Clear, as the design labels it.\n\n`requiredDates` draws the mark beside each label. It is deliberately\ndecorative — no `aria-required` on the inputs — because the filter needs one\nof the two dates, not both, and announcing them as required would be wrong.\n\nBoth props reach the table through DateRangeFilterConfig. The Created story\nnow sets `revealCalendarOnCustom`, which is what the design''s written spec\ndescribes: the calendar opens from the Custom range option.\n\n* 🐛 fix: reconcile the date range picker with the filter''s written behaviour\n\nTwo points where the picker contradicted the spec the design carries.\n\nAn inverted pair was rejected: the field showed an ordering error and both\ndates were discarded, throwing away work the user had already done. The spec\nsays the two are simply swapped, which is what the calendar has always done\nwhen a day before the start is picked — only the typed fields disagreed. The\ntwo ordering messages have no code path left and are gone.\n\nAnd the picker always opened with a preset selected, so a filter showed its\ncalendar before anything had been chosen. `defaultPreset` now accepts `null`\nfor no selection, which the filter uses until a range is applied; paired with\nprogressive disclosure the dropdown opens on the preset list alone. Clearing\nremounts the picker, since it seeds itself from `defaultRange` on mount and\nwould otherwise keep displaying the range that was just discarded.\n\n* ✅ test: show the Created date range filter inside the table''s filter row\n\nThe `dateRange` filter type had no table story, and the placement is the whole\npoint of it: the control belongs in the row beside the search box and Reset,\nnot as a separate widget above the table. The story wires the design''s three\nrolling presets plus a custom range that opens the calendar, applying as soon\nas a preset is picked and refusing dates in the future.\n\n* 💄 refactor: keep the range filter actions with the calendar they act on\n\nA preset applies the moment it is picked, so on the bare preset list Apply had\nnothing left to do and Clear sat under a menu the design draws without either:\nthe Menu node carries the four options and nothing else. The footer now appears\nwith the calendar, which is what it acts on.\n\nWithout progressive disclosure the calendar is always up, so the buttons are\ntoo, and that path is unchanged.\n\nOne consequence worth naming: while a preset is applied and the calendar is\nclosed, this filter no longer offers its own Clear, and the reset that clears\nit is the table''s.\n\n* 🐛 fix: do not open the picker on a month nobody can select\n\nThe picker always opened on the reference month plus the one after it. For a\ncreation-date filter capped at today that spends the right-hand calendar on a\nmonth entirely in the future, every day disabled — opening on 1 September\nshowed August alongside a dead October.\n\nThe pair now ends on the last month with a selectable day in it and steps back\nfrom there, so the reference month stays in view: the same filter opens on\nAugust and September. Without a maxDate, or with one further out, nothing\nchanges.\n\nThe computation moved into `getDisplayedMonths`, where it can be tested\ndirectly — the calendar keeps adjacent months mounted as carousel slides, so\nmonth captions cannot be told apart from the rendered output.\n\n* ✅ test: let the table story actually reload behind the Created filter\n\nThe story handed the table a static list, so applying the filter changed\nnothing on screen and the example said little. It goes through `fetchData`\nnow, which the table already calls with the applied window under the filter''s\nkey, and it carries a Created column to look at: picking \"Last 7 days\" takes\nthe fixture from 500 rows to 76.\n\nThe pokemon fixture has no creation date, so the story derives one per row\nfrom its id, spread over the last seven weeks. Those dates and the filter''s\ncap share one midnight, so a row created today is never past `maxDate`.\n\n* 💄 refactor: keep clear reachable while a range filter is applied\n\nHiding the whole footer on the bare preset list took Clear with it, so a filter\napplied from a preset could only be taken off through the table''s own reset —\nand the preset list is where the user comes back to take it off.\n\nClear now shows whenever something is applied. Apply does not: a preset applies\nthe moment it is picked, so on that list it would have nothing left to do.\n\n* 🐛 fix: never strand a preset selection with no way to apply it\n\nTying the footer to the calendar left a dead end. With progressive disclosure\non and `applyOnPresetSelect` off, picking a rolling preset fills the selection,\nresolves to a window so the calendar stays shut, and applies nothing — and the\napply button was hidden, so that choice could never reach the table.\n\nApply now follows the work it would commit rather than the calendar: it shows\nwhile a selection is pending, meaning one the user has made that differs from\nwhat is applied. That is deliberately not `canApply`, which stays true after a\npreset applies itself and would put the button back where it has nothing left\nto do.\n\n* ✅ test: show the range filter where apply is pressed by hand\n\nThe pending-selection path had no story, so the button it restores could not be\nseen: every existing story sets `applyOnPresetSelect`, where a preset commits\nitself and apply correctly stays away.\n\nThis one leaves that off. Picking a preset checks it, keeps the dropdown open\nand offers apply — which before was missing, stranding the choice.\n\n* ♻️ refactor: move preset-driven range filter into CustomDateRangeFilterDropdown\n\nDateRangeFilterDropdown goes back to its shape on main. The custom presets,\nprogressive calendar disclosure, apply-on-preset behaviour and From/To/Clear\nwording now live in a new Filter.CustomDateRangeFilterDropdown, exposed to\nVirtualizedTable as the customDateRange filter type.\n\n* ♻️ refactor: split CustomDateRangeFilterDropdown hook into atomic hooks\n\nuseCustomDateRangeFilterDropdown owned seven concerns at once: open state,\nselected range, applied range, badge label formatting, pending-selection\ndiffing, calendar visibility and the reset key. Each now lives in its own\nhook under hooks/<name>/ with its own types file, none of them importing\nanother hook. The component composes them and keeps the three cross-cutting\nflows (apply, preset change, clear) plus the closeOnApply handling that used\nto be buried inside the hooks.\n\n* ♻️ refactor: rename Filter subcomponent prop types to Props\n\nFollows the repo convention of exporting Props from each *.types.ts and\ntyping components as FC<Props>. The components barrel now re-exports each\none under its former public alias (BadgeMultiSelectProps,\nDateFilterDropdownProps, ...), so Filter.types.ts and tests import them\nunchanged.\n\n* 📝 docs: add props ordering rule and apply it to CustomDateRangeFilterDropdown\n\nProps types list data properties alphabetically first, then callbacks and\nother function-typed props alphabetically at the end.\n\n* ✨ feat: forward onClick to the Radio input\n\nThe prop was already part of Props through InputHTMLAttributes but never\nreached the input. A checked radio fires no change event when clicked\nagain, so onClick is the only way for a consumer to react to that click.\n\n* ✨ feat: let RadioGroup clear its selection with a null value\n\nPassing undefined switches the group to uncontrolled mode, which brings\nback whatever it last stored internally. null now means \"controlled, with\nnothing selected\", so a consumer can deselect every option.\n\n* 💄 style: keep the preset separator clear of the row hover\n\nThe rule above the trailing manual-selection row sat on the row''s own top\nedge, so its hover fill ran right up to the line. The rule now lives in the\nrow''s top margin, centred in the gap with the same 10px on either side that\nthe panel keeps at its sides. The text does not move.\n\n* ✨ feat: animate the calendar reveal and let the custom range collapse\n\nRightPanel moves into its own component and becomes a motion.div inside\nAnimatePresence: with revealCalendarOnCustom it grows in width and height\nfrom the preset column and folds back the same way, timed by the picker''s\nanimationDuration. Nothing animates on first mount.\n\nThe trailing manual-selection row now reads as a disclosure. While its\ncalendar is open it keeps the hover fill and its chevron turns aurora and\nrotates 180°; clicking it again sets the preset to null and folds the\ncalendar. onPresetChange and setPreset accept null for that, and\nrevealCalendarOnCustom lives in the picker context so PresetPanel and\nRightPanel can both act on it. The chevron also turns aurora on hover.\n\n* ✨ feat: add a footer slot to the DateRangePicker right panel\n\nContent passed as `footer` renders under the calendar, inside the animated\nright column, so a consumer can put its actions there and the preset column\nstretches to the full height of the picker.\n\n* 📝 docs: allow implicit JSX return for logic-free components\n\nA component with no hooks, variables or handlers that only returns JSX may\nuse a parenthesised body; everything else keeps the block body rule.\n\n* ♻️ refactor: drive CustomDateRangeFilterDropdown from a context\n\nThe component was a single function juggling twenty props and every\nhandler. It now passes its props to a provider and renders Wrapper, which\ncomposes Trigger, Picker and the two action rows, each reading only what it\nneeds from the context. The provider applies the defaults, composes the\natomic hooks and owns the three cross-cutting flows (apply, preset change,\nclear) together with the closeOnApply handling.\n\nBehaviour that comes with the new layout:\n\n- Clear and Apply live inside the picker''s footer while the calendar is\n  open (PanelActions), so they fold and unfold with it and the preset\n  column border reaches the bottom of the dropdown, as in the design. The\n  standalone row (Actions) only shows while the calendar is folded: Clear\n  alone once a filter is applied, plus Apply for a preset chosen without\n  applyOnPresetSelect. It animates its height with the panel and fades its\n  buttons in once the panel has finished growing.\n- Folding the custom range hides its actions but keeps the pending dates;\n  useCalendarVisibility tracks that folded state so it is not mistaken for\n  a pending preset that still needs Apply.\n- Clear inside the calendar empties the inputs and drops the applied range\n  without closing the dropdown or folding the calendar. The folded-state\n  Clear keeps resetting and closing.\n- Picker derives defaultPreset from the filter''s own calendar state, so\n  reopening after a windowed preset no longer lands on an expanded custom\n  range.\n- animationDuration is accepted and shared between the picker and the\n  action rows. The tests set it to 0 so the fold assertions do not depend\n  on jsdom animation timing.\n\n* ✅ test: run the calendar fold checks without animation delays\n\nUnder the load of the full suite the 500ms exit animation could outlast\nwaitFor''s default timeout. The reveal tests now pass animationDuration 0.\n\n* ✨ feat: add single-month, numeric-date and paired-month options to the DateRangePicker\n\n- `numberOfMonths` (1 | 2) renders a single 259px month with shared prev/next\n  navigation, as the Product Design System draws it (Figma node 2473-2654).\n  The initial month and the `maxDate` cap are computed against the visible\n  month rather than the pair, and the From/To inputs collapse into one compact\n  row (or stack when time inputs are on).\n- `dateDisplayFormat` (''long'' | ''numeric'') chooses between \"3 September 2026\"\n  and \"09/03/2026\" in the date inputs, at rest and after blur.\n- The preset column''s right rule fades to transparent while the right panel is\n  folded, over the same duration as the fold, so it no longer doubles the\n  dropdown edge.\n- Provider props move to `date-range-picker.types.ts` as a type alias, and the\n  `hidesRightPanel` decision is shared between RightPanel and PresetPanel.\n- `constants/`, `contexts/` and `utils/` now hold one folder per module behind\n  their barrel, `DateRestrictions` and `PresetOption` live in `.types.ts`\n  files, and every inline `if` body is wrapped in braces.\n- Stories: SingleMonth and NumericDates in Dark and Light.\n\n* ♻️ refactor: move the Filter dropdown hooks into hooks folders\n\nEach `*.hook.ts` beside a dropdown becomes `hooks/<useX>/<useX>.ts` behind a\n`hooks/index.ts` barrel, matching the layout the rest of the filter uses.\n\n* ✨ feat: make the custom date range filter''s presets, layout and badge configurable\n\nPresets\n- Built-in rolling presets (Last 24 hours / 7 days / 30 days) plus a custom\n  range, so `presets` is no longer required. `rollingPresets` takes an array of\n  `{ label, duration, value? }`, where `duration` reads like \"30m\", \"1h\",\n  \"7days\", \"2months\" or \"10years\": fixed units subtract milliseconds, months and\n  years step back on the calendar and clamp to the shorter month. Ids derive\n  from the duration (\"last-7d\", \"last-2mo\") when `value` is omitted.\n- `showCustomRange={false}` drops the manual entry and never shows the\n  calendar; `labelCustomRange` renames it.\n\nSelection state\n- The applied preset stays marked when the dropdown reopens, and a picked day\n  marks the custom entry.\n- Closing without applying discards the pending selection and returns to the\n  last applied state.\n- Apply is disabled until the selection differs from what was applied.\n- `defaultRange` counts as applied, so the trigger shows it from the start.\n- Manual picks without time span whole days: from 00:00:00.000 to 23:59:59.999.\n\nPresentation\n- `appliedRangeDisplay=\"split\"` shows each end of the applied range in its own\n  badge joined by `labelRangeSeparator` (\"to\"), per Figma node 1564-10782.\n- `numberOfMonths`, `navigationMode`, `showOutsideDays` and `dateDisplayFormat`\n  pass through to the picker.\n\nStructure\n- Context files, constants, hooks and utils each sit in their own folder behind\n  a barrel; new hooks `usePresetOptions` and `useSelectedPreset`, utils\n  `duration` and `whole-days`.\n\nStories: Created stories rely on the defaults, `CreatedDateRangeSplitBadge`\nshows the split trigger, and `DateRangeVariations` lines up single month,\npresets only, custom rolling windows and paired months in one bar.\n\n* ✨ feat: forward every custom date range option to the table filter\n\n`CustomDateRangeFilterConfig` now derives from the dropdown''s own props instead\nof a hand-copied list, and the table''s Filter spreads the config into the\ndropdown, so new options reach `filters` without touching the table.\n\nThe `CreatedDateRangeFilter` story grows synthetic Updated, Expires and Deleted\ncolumns and filters, each in a different shape: single month with a split badge,\npresets only, custom rolling windows, and paired months with numeric inputs.\n`fetchData` narrows the rows by every applied window.\n\n* 📝 docs: require braces on every if body\n\nExtends the early-return rule to any `if`/`else` body, and asks to convert\ninline ones found in files being edited.\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Jairo Fernandez <jairo@civo.com>\nCo-authored-by: Jesús Manuel Fuentes Trejo <futjesus@gmail.com>","tree":{"sha":"60df4e9f7b12da887356988f28fdb41a1f3e1a62","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/konstructio/konstruct-ui/git/trees/60df4e9f7b12da887356988f28fdb41a1f3e1a62"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/konstructio/konstruct-ui/git/commits/a43c03d53b442bb9d256478dcad7217c28dd0d02","verification":{"payload":"tree 60df4e9f7b12da887356988f28fdb41a1f3e1a62\nparent 05441266a867c91096ef9ddfd37e14cee3ea073c\nauthor Jairo Fernández <jairofg12@gmail.com> 1788447981 -0500\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788447981 -0400\n\nfeat(DateRangePicker): consumer-supplied presets, opt-in apply-on-select and progressive disclosure (#710)\n\n* ✨ feat: let DateRangePicker consumers supply their own presets\n\nThe preset panel offered five hardcoded options, and the range each one stood\nfor lived in a separate switch in `calculatePresetRange`. A consumer could\nrename them through `presetLabels` but not change what they meant, so a design\nasking for rolling windows — \"Last 24 hours\", \"the past 7×24 hours\" — could not\nbe expressed at all: `current-month` and `today` are calendar buckets, and\n`last-7-days` is seven whole days ending at midnight.\n\nAn option now owns both halves. `presets` takes a list of\n`{ value, label, resolve(now) }`, the built-ins carry their own resolvers, and\nthe switch is gone — the panel and the resolution can no longer drift apart.\nPassing `now` in is what makes a rolling window expressible.\n\n`custom` stays the reserved id the calendar switches back to when a day is\npicked; an option whose `resolve` returns an empty range is treated as that\nmanual-selection entry, so a consumer''s list keeps working without knowing the\ninternals. Array order is render order, which also lets \"Custom range\" sit last.\n\n`DateRangePreset` widens to accept any id while keeping autocompletion for the\nfive built-ins, so no existing consumer breaks. `presetLabels` still works and\nis documented as superseded by `presets`.\n\nAlso forwards `presets` through `VirtualizedTable`''s `dateRange` filter config\nand `DateRangeFilterDropdown`. Without that the table — the main way this picker\nis consumed — would still be stuck with the built-in five.\n\nTests cover the new prop (supplied options replace the built-ins, render order,\nresolution through the option''s own `resolve`, `now` being passed, the\nempty-range option behaving as manual selection, `defaultPreset` resolving\nagainst a supplied list) plus a guard that the built-in windows are unchanged\nafter moving them out of the switch. A RollingPresets story is added to both\nthemes.\n\n* ✨ feat: opt-in apply-on-preset and labels for the date range filter\n\nFollow-up to the presets prop, from reviewing a product''s local reimplementation\nof this filter against the design it was built to. Three of its five departures\nfrom the library were left alone on purpose: closing on apply already exists as\n`closeOnApply`, an inverted range is a deliberate error message here\n(`errorStartAfterEnd`) rather than a silent swap, and progressively revealing the\ncalendar would restructure this component''s layout. Only the additive parts are\ntaken.\n\n- `onPresetChange(preset, range)` on DateRangePicker: fires whenever a preset is\n  chosen, including a manual-selection entry resolving to nothing. `onRangeChange`\n  cannot distinguish \"chose a preset\" from \"clicked a day\", and a consumer needs\n  that distinction to react to one and not the other. Purely additive.\n\n- `applyOnPresetSelect` on the filter dropdown (default false, so nothing\n  changes): applies and closes as soon as a preset is picked, while the calendar\n  still waits for the button. A design that treats presets as one-click shortcuts\n  can opt in.\n\n- The apply button is disabled while nothing is selected. Applying an empty range\n  filtered nothing and reported a range that was never chosen; clearing is what\n  the reset button is for.\n\n- `labelApply` / `labelReset` on the dropdown and the table''s filter config. Both\n  strings were hardcoded English in a library shipped to several products, so\n  this is as much an i18n gap as a wording one.\n\nAll four are forwarded through VirtualizedTable''s `dateRange` filter config.\n\n* ✨ feat: opt-in progressive disclosure for the date range calendar\n\nThird and last idea taken from a product''s local reimplementation of this\nfilter: presets as one-click shortcuts, with the calendar revealed only when the\nuser asks for a custom range. Reviewing that side by side, its dropdown reads\nbetter than always showing two months for a choice most users make with a\npreset — so the behaviour is offered rather than imposed. Default is unchanged.\n\n- `revealCalendarOnCustom` on DateRangePicker (default false): the date inputs\n  and calendar render only while the manual-selection preset is active — the\n  entry whose `resolve` returns an empty range, `custom` by default. Keyed on\n  the resolved range rather than the literal id, so a consumer''s own preset list\n  works without knowing the internals.\n\n  Pair it with a `defaultPreset` that resolves to a window: the library defaults\n  to `custom`, which would otherwise open expanded. Documented on the prop.\n\n- A `RightPanel` subcomponent now wraps the inputs + calendar. Whether the\n  active preset resolves to a window is only knowable from context, and the\n  provider is created by DateRangePicker itself, so the decision has to live in\n  a child.\n\n- Forwarded through the filter dropdown and VirtualizedTable''s `dateRange`\n  config, plus `labelTimePeriod`, which the dropdown accepted nowhere before —\n  the panel title was stuck on \"Time period\" with no way to say \"Created\".\n\nTests cover both states and the transitions in each direction, plus that the\npresets stay visible while the calendar is hidden and that the default keeps the\ncalendar on screen. A RevealOnCustom story is added to both themes, and the\nfilter dropdown gets its first story (CreatedDateRange) so the assembled control\ncan be reviewed rather than only its parts.\n\n* 💄 refactor: align the date range preset panel with the design system\n\nThe preset column was a translucent card floating inside the picker: it\ncarried its own fill and rounded corners, and sat inset from the edge by the\ncontainer''s padding. The design has one continuous surface split by hairlines,\nso the padding moves from the container onto each column and the gap between\nthem becomes a border. The container already declared a border colour it never\ndrew; that is now the token both rules use.\n\nThe options themselves read as a list rather than a set of radios. The control\nis hidden instead of removed, so the rows keep their radio semantics and\nkeyboard handling, and selection shows as a trailing check. A manual-selection\noption that closes the list is marked with a chevron and ruled off from the\nrolling presets above it — the rule is a pseudo-element, since it runs\nfull-bleed while the row''s hover fill stays inset.\n\nSizes come from the Figma node: a fixed 206px column so the calendar beside it\ndoes not shift with the labels, 24px to the text, a 36px row on a 40px pitch,\nand 20px icons.\n\n* ✨ feat: From/To labels and required marks for the date range filter\n\nThe filter dropdown never forwarded the picker''s date labels, so a consumer\nhad no way to reach the From/To wording the design asks for inside a filter.\nIt forwards them now and defaults to that wording, and the reset action is\ncalled Clear, as the design labels it.\n\n`requiredDates` draws the mark beside each label. It is deliberately\ndecorative — no `aria-required` on the inputs — because the filter needs one\nof the two dates, not both, and announcing them as required would be wrong.\n\nBoth props reach the table through DateRangeFilterConfig. The Created story\nnow sets `revealCalendarOnCustom`, which is what the design''s written spec\ndescribes: the calendar opens from the Custom range option.\n\n* 🐛 fix: reconcile the date range picker with the filter''s written behaviour\n\nTwo points where the picker contradicted the spec the design carries.\n\nAn inverted pair was rejected: the field showed an ordering error and both\ndates were discarded, throwing away work the user had already done. The spec\nsays the two are simply swapped, which is what the calendar has always done\nwhen a day before the start is picked — only the typed fields disagreed. The\ntwo ordering messages have no code path left and are gone.\n\nAnd the picker always opened with a preset selected, so a filter showed its\ncalendar before anything had been chosen. `defaultPreset` now accepts `null`\nfor no selection, which the filter uses until a range is applied; paired with\nprogressive disclosure the dropdown opens on the preset list alone. Clearing\nremounts the picker, since it seeds itself from `defaultRange` on mount and\nwould otherwise keep displaying the range that was just discarded.\n\n* ✅ test: show the Created date range filter inside the table''s filter row\n\nThe `dateRange` filter type had no table story, and the placement is the whole\npoint of it: the control belongs in the row beside the search box and Reset,\nnot as a separate widget above the table. The story wires the design''s three\nrolling presets plus a custom range that opens the calendar, applying as soon\nas a preset is picked and refusing dates in the future.\n\n* 💄 refactor: keep the range filter actions with the calendar they act on\n\nA preset applies the moment it is picked, so on the bare preset list Apply had\nnothing left to do and Clear sat under a menu the design draws without either:\nthe Menu node carries the four options and nothing else. The footer now appears\nwith the calendar, which is what it acts on.\n\nWithout progressive disclosure the calendar is always up, so the buttons are\ntoo, and that path is unchanged.\n\nOne consequence worth naming: while a preset is applied and the calendar is\nclosed, this filter no longer offers its own Clear, and the reset that clears\nit is the table''s.\n\n* 🐛 fix: do not open the picker on a month nobody can select\n\nThe picker always opened on the reference month plus the one after it. For a\ncreation-date filter capped at today that spends the right-hand calendar on a\nmonth entirely in the future, every day disabled — opening on 1 September\nshowed August alongside a dead October.\n\nThe pair now ends on the last month with a selectable day in it and steps back\nfrom there, so the reference month stays in view: the same filter opens on\nAugust and September. Without a maxDate, or with one further out, nothing\nchanges.\n\nThe computation moved into `getDisplayedMonths`, where it can be tested\ndirectly — the calendar keeps adjacent months mounted as carousel slides, so\nmonth captions cannot be told apart from the rendered output.\n\n* ✅ test: let the table story actually reload behind the Created filter\n\nThe story handed the table a static list, so applying the filter changed\nnothing on screen and the example said little. It goes through `fetchData`\nnow, which the table already calls with the applied window under the filter''s\nkey, and it carries a Created column to look at: picking \"Last 7 days\" takes\nthe fixture from 500 rows to 76.\n\nThe pokemon fixture has no creation date, so the story derives one per row\nfrom its id, spread over the last seven weeks. Those dates and the filter''s\ncap share one midnight, so a row created today is never past `maxDate`.\n\n* 💄 refactor: keep clear reachable while a range filter is applied\n\nHiding the whole footer on the bare preset list took Clear with it, so a filter\napplied from a preset could only be taken off through the table''s own reset —\nand the preset list is where the user comes back to take it off.\n\nClear now shows whenever something is applied. Apply does not: a preset applies\nthe moment it is picked, so on that list it would have nothing left to do.\n\n* 🐛 fix: never strand a preset selection with no way to apply it\n\nTying the footer to the calendar left a dead end. With progressive disclosure\non and `applyOnPresetSelect` off, picking a rolling preset fills the selection,\nresolves to a window so the calendar stays shut, and applies nothing — and the\napply button was hidden, so that choice could never reach the table.\n\nApply now follows the work it would commit rather than the calendar: it shows\nwhile a selection is pending, meaning one the user has made that differs from\nwhat is applied. That is deliberately not `canApply`, which stays true after a\npreset applies itself and would put the button back where it has nothing left\nto do.\n\n* ✅ test: show the range filter where apply is pressed by hand\n\nThe pending-selection path had no story, so the button it restores could not be\nseen: every existing story sets `applyOnPresetSelect`, where a preset commits\nitself and apply correctly stays away.\n\nThis one leaves that off. Picking a preset checks it, keeps the dropdown open\nand offers apply — which before was missing, stranding the choice.\n\n* ♻️ refactor: move preset-driven range filter into CustomDateRangeFilterDropdown\n\nDateRangeFilterDropdown goes back to its shape on main. The custom presets,\nprogressive calendar disclosure, apply-on-preset behaviour and From/To/Clear\nwording now live in a new Filter.CustomDateRangeFilterDropdown, exposed to\nVirtualizedTable as the customDateRange filter type.\n\n* ♻️ refactor: split CustomDateRangeFilterDropdown hook into atomic hooks\n\nuseCustomDateRangeFilterDropdown owned seven concerns at once: open state,\nselected range, applied range, badge label formatting, pending-selection\ndiffing, calendar visibility and the reset key. Each now lives in its own\nhook under hooks/<name>/ with its own types file, none of them importing\nanother hook. The component composes them and keeps the three cross-cutting\nflows (apply, preset change, clear) plus the closeOnApply handling that used\nto be buried inside the hooks.\n\n* ♻️ refactor: rename Filter subcomponent prop types to Props\n\nFollows the repo convention of exporting Props from each *.types.ts and\ntyping components as FC<Props>. The components barrel now re-exports each\none under its former public alias (BadgeMultiSelectProps,\nDateFilterDropdownProps, ...), so Filter.types.ts and tests import them\nunchanged.\n\n* 📝 docs: add props ordering rule and apply it to CustomDateRangeFilterDropdown\n\nProps types list data properties alphabetically first, then callbacks and\nother function-typed props alphabetically at the end.\n\n* ✨ feat: forward onClick to the Radio input\n\nThe prop was already part of Props through InputHTMLAttributes but never\nreached the input. A checked radio fires no change event when clicked\nagain, so onClick is the only way for a consumer to react to that click.\n\n* ✨ feat: let RadioGroup clear its selection with a null value\n\nPassing undefined switches the group to uncontrolled mode, which brings\nback whatever it last stored internally. null now means \"controlled, with\nnothing selected\", so a consumer can deselect every option.\n\n* 💄 style: keep the preset separator clear of the row hover\n\nThe rule above the trailing manual-selection row sat on the row''s own top\nedge, so its hover fill ran right up to the line. The rule now lives in the\nrow''s top margin, centred in the gap with the same 10px on either side that\nthe panel keeps at its sides. The text does not move.\n\n* ✨ feat: animate the calendar reveal and let the custom range collapse\n\nRightPanel moves into its own component and becomes a motion.div inside\nAnimatePresence: with revealCalendarOnCustom it grows in width and height\nfrom the preset column and folds back the same way, timed by the picker''s\nanimationDuration. Nothing animates on first mount.\n\nThe trailing manual-selection row now reads as a disclosure. While its\ncalendar is open it keeps the hover fill and its chevron turns aurora and\nrotates 180°; clicking it again sets the preset to null and folds the\ncalendar. onPresetChange and setPreset accept null for that, and\nrevealCalendarOnCustom lives in the picker context so PresetPanel and\nRightPanel can both act on it. The chevron also turns aurora on hover.\n\n* ✨ feat: add a footer slot to the DateRangePicker right panel\n\nContent passed as `footer` renders under the calendar, inside the animated\nright column, so a consumer can put its actions there and the preset column\nstretches to the full height of the picker.\n\n* 📝 docs: allow implicit JSX return for logic-free components\n\nA component with no hooks, variables or handlers that only returns JSX may\nuse a parenthesised body; everything else keeps the block body rule.\n\n* ♻️ refactor: drive CustomDateRangeFilterDropdown from a context\n\nThe component was a single function juggling twenty props and every\nhandler. It now passes its props to a provider and renders Wrapper, which\ncomposes Trigger, Picker and the two action rows, each reading only what it\nneeds from the context. The provider applies the defaults, composes the\natomic hooks and owns the three cross-cutting flows (apply, preset change,\nclear) together with the closeOnApply handling.\n\nBehaviour that comes with the new layout:\n\n- Clear and Apply live inside the picker''s footer while the calendar is\n  open (PanelActions), so they fold and unfold with it and the preset\n  column border reaches the bottom of the dropdown, as in the design. The\n  standalone row (Actions) only shows while the calendar is folded: Clear\n  alone once a filter is applied, plus Apply for a preset chosen without\n  applyOnPresetSelect. It animates its height with the panel and fades its\n  buttons in once the panel has finished growing.\n- Folding the custom range hides its actions but keeps the pending dates;\n  useCalendarVisibility tracks that folded state so it is not mistaken for\n  a pending preset that still needs Apply.\n- Clear inside the calendar empties the inputs and drops the applied range\n  without closing the dropdown or folding the calendar. The folded-state\n  Clear keeps resetting and closing.\n- Picker derives defaultPreset from the filter''s own calendar state, so\n  reopening after a windowed preset no longer lands on an expanded custom\n  range.\n- animationDuration is accepted and shared between the picker and the\n  action rows. The tests set it to 0 so the fold assertions do not depend\n  on jsdom animation timing.\n\n* ✅ test: run the calendar fold checks without animation delays\n\nUnder the load of the full suite the 500ms exit animation could outlast\nwaitFor''s default timeout. The reveal tests now pass animationDuration 0.\n\n* ✨ feat: add single-month, numeric-date and paired-month options to the DateRangePicker\n\n- `numberOfMonths` (1 | 2) renders a single 259px month with shared prev/next\n  navigation, as the Product Design System draws it (Figma node 2473-2654).\n  The initial month and the `maxDate` cap are computed against the visible\n  month rather than the pair, and the From/To inputs collapse into one compact\n  row (or stack when time inputs are on).\n- `dateDisplayFormat` (''long'' | ''numeric'') chooses between \"3 September 2026\"\n  and \"09/03/2026\" in the date inputs, at rest and after blur.\n- The preset column''s right rule fades to transparent while the right panel is\n  folded, over the same duration as the fold, so it no longer doubles the\n  dropdown edge.\n- Provider props move to `date-range-picker.types.ts` as a type alias, and the\n  `hidesRightPanel` decision is shared between RightPanel and PresetPanel.\n- `constants/`, `contexts/` and `utils/` now hold one folder per module behind\n  their barrel, `DateRestrictions` and `PresetOption` live in `.types.ts`\n  files, and every inline `if` body is wrapped in braces.\n- Stories: SingleMonth and NumericDates in Dark and Light.\n\n* ♻️ refactor: move the Filter dropdown hooks into hooks folders\n\nEach `*.hook.ts` beside a dropdown becomes `hooks/<useX>/<useX>.ts` behind a\n`hooks/index.ts` barrel, matching the layout the rest of the filter uses.\n\n* ✨ feat: make the custom date range filter''s presets, layout and badge configurable\n\nPresets\n- Built-in rolling presets (Last 24 hours / 7 days / 30 days) plus a custom\n  range, so `presets` is no longer required. `rollingPresets` takes an array of\n  `{ label, duration, value? }`, where `duration` reads like \"30m\", \"1h\",\n  \"7days\", \"2months\" or \"10years\": fixed units subtract milliseconds, months and\n  years step back on the calendar and clamp to the shorter month. Ids derive\n  from the duration (\"last-7d\", \"last-2mo\") when `value` is omitted.\n- `showCustomRange={false}` drops the manual entry and never shows the\n  calendar; `labelCustomRange` renames it.\n\nSelection state\n- The applied preset stays marked when the dropdown reopens, and a picked day\n  marks the custom entry.\n- Closing without applying discards the pending selection and returns to the\n  last applied state.\n- Apply is disabled until the selection differs from what was applied.\n- `defaultRange` counts as applied, so the trigger shows it from the start.\n- Manual picks without time span whole days: from 00:00:00.000 to 23:59:59.999.\n\nPresentation\n- `appliedRangeDisplay=\"split\"` shows each end of the applied range in its own\n  badge joined by `labelRangeSeparator` (\"to\"), per Figma node 1564-10782.\n- `numberOfMonths`, `navigationMode`, `showOutsideDays` and `dateDisplayFormat`\n  pass through to the picker.\n\nStructure\n- Context files, constants, hooks and utils each sit in their own folder behind\n  a barrel; new hooks `usePresetOptions` and `useSelectedPreset`, utils\n  `duration` and `whole-days`.\n\nStories: Created stories rely on the defaults, `CreatedDateRangeSplitBadge`\nshows the split trigger, and `DateRangeVariations` lines up single month,\npresets only, custom rolling windows and paired months in one bar.\n\n* ✨ feat: forward every custom date range option to the table filter\n\n`CustomDateRangeFilterConfig` now derives from the dropdown''s own props instead\nof a hand-copied list, and the table''s Filter spreads the config into the\ndropdown, so new options reach `filters` without touching the table.\n\nThe `CreatedDateRangeFilter` story grows synthetic Updated, Expires and Deleted\ncolumns and filters, each in a different shape: single month with a split badge,\npresets only, custom rolling windows, and paired months with numeric inputs.\n`fetchData` narrows the rows by every applied window.\n\n* 📝 docs: require braces on every if body\n\nExtends the early-return rule to any `if`/`else` body, and asks to convert\ninline ones found in files being edited.\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Jairo Fernandez <jairo@civo.com>\nCo-authored-by: Jesús Manuel Fuentes Trejo <futjesus@gmail.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqmYztCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAr5sQAEJITroowf4BaJbNdusHdOtn\nMnUB01gD9V+Q3+BFfgc0fJHrVWkCraE8eTD3+578VliSvXatBmIyFGXiZT3JOaaT\n3EbjreVSOb6bezNmMlG/wXNCBFXnPVvgWLMVp1Xn6d2/E/bYm/0d8MfzilObbgGG\nb4v+f9+0We6e+rvdm2/ud8HqlUE6qd4AS/xdr7UzYoGbKM4uFvUM0W3bq0RGJjeI\nUrCD/IqUWZV/yZ4IAQTen4nQitn8ROxTRyxdjK7ikUia+mPNUt3ep+9pbiw2BzqG\nUBtPDWhV+/xjOOMQn097Mj8BVG8XPYCqXjzq/xfsA7r2URg2djl/Typ0AVWIean2\nPIh/hb7RhWW97ntMRoVRPR+UKc/q2HX5fYaRDwK7+R/IVxT2LHiz3gjKUGKLUzIn\nglfwYQ9VVDMqge7M8agP4uCBRq4iS1wmJe6nWQv+olwTWrcZjJhnq6q9kBwhNjOs\nmtDFkpCB4W8twCKRTB88CyEMy4IRGkzeZa9wFMBFbcSInnO/+0RKQSl0Gl0bVj76\niuJJf/AG3O/i/UWqLcbaLpJE5fMCAwPUgqyihT5Q5pntL3zEO+S+hHzIQtKQnDSb\na1KBR2KMRoOEpp737/vV8xktWFmysld4OgX9/wFiFhJ96XRKTod81LDLKRMficNd\n0vqsay8WNmin886oB7un\n=TsMS\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-03T15:06:22Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","follow